# Dev Bug Fix Rules

## 🚫 Core Rule

**Never fix a bug unless the user explicitly says to fix it.**

- If you find a bug: **report** it (see `.cursor/rules/bug-reporting.mdc`), do **not** fix.
- Document in `Testing/Automation_Testing/bugs/BUGS.md` or `Testing/Functional and Unit testing/bugs/BUGS.md` per bug-reporting rule.
- Wait for explicit user approval before making any production code changes.

---

## 1️⃣ Understand Before Changing

Always read the entire file before modifying.

Search for:

Where the function is used

Related test cases

Environment configs

Explain root cause before suggesting a fix.

Never change code without explaining:

Why it is failing

Why the fix works

## 2️⃣ Root Cause First (No Guess Fixes)

Do not apply patch-based guessing.

Reproduce issue logically from:

Logs

Stack trace

Error message

If information is missing, ask for:

Exact error

Expected vs actual behavior

Steps to reproduce

## 3️⃣ Minimal & Safe Changes

Modify only the required lines.

Do not refactor unrelated code.

Preserve:

Existing architecture

Coding style

Naming conventions

Avoid introducing new dependencies unless necessary.

## 4️⃣ Backward Compatibility

Ensure fix does not break:

Existing APIs

Existing test cases

Public interfaces

If breaking change is unavoidable:

Clearly explain impact.

## 5️⃣ Add/Update Tests

For every bug fix:

- Add at least: 1 positive test, 1 negative test, 1 edge case test.
- If test already exists: Fix test only if it is incorrect. **Do NOT weaken assertions.**
- Tests live under `Testing/Automation_Testing/` or `Testing/End2End_Automation/` (see automation-testing.mdc).
- After fix: Run the previously failing test; it must pass **without changing the test**.

## 6️⃣ Logging & Debugging

Add meaningful logs (not excessive).

Never log:

Passwords

Secrets

Tokens

Use existing logging framework only.

## 7️⃣ Code Quality Rules

Follow project lint rules.

No hardcoded values.

No commented dead code.

No TODO left unresolved.

Use constants instead of magic numbers.

## 8️⃣ Performance & Security Check

Before final answer, Cursor must verify:

No O(n²) performance issue introduced.

No null pointer risks.

Input validation present.

No SQL injection risk.

No exposed secrets.

## 9️⃣ Clear Explanation Format

Every fix must follow this format:

✅ Root Cause:
<Explain issue>

✅ Fix:
<Explain change>

✅ Why This Works:
<Technical reasoning>

✅ Side Effects:
<Any impact>

✅ Tests Added:
<List test scenarios>

## 🔟 If Multiple Files Are Affected

List all impacted files first.

Explain change file-by-file.

Provide patch-style output.

## 🚀 Advanced Cursor Behavior Rules (For Large Projects)
When Fixing Bugs:

Prefer reading call hierarchy first.

Trace execution flow from entry point.

Check config/env impact.

Check recent commits logic (if available).

NEVER Allow Cursor To:

❌ Rewrite full file unnecessarily
❌ Change working test cases
❌ Introduce new patterns randomly
❌ Assume framework behavior without checking docs
❌ Use outdated library syntax

